.SH NOTES
In child (session) processes, signal handlers are reset to defaults
after \f[CR]fork()\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
signal(SIGCHLD, SIG_DFL);
signal(SIGINT, SIG_DFL);
signal(SIGTERM, SIG_DFL);
.EE
.PP
This ensures that session processes respond to signals normally rather
than setting flags intended for the parent\(cqs accept loop.
.PP
The \f[CR]server_running\f[R], \f[CR]got_sigchld\f[R], and
\f[CR]active_clients\f[R] variables are file\-scoped (\f[CR]static\f[R])
in \f[CR]server_main.c\f[R].
The handler modifies only \f[CR]server_running\f[R] and
\f[CR]got_sigchld\f[R].
.PP
The \f[CR]SIGPIPE\f[R] case in the switch statement is a defensive
no\-op.
In practice, \f[CR]SIGPIPE\f[R] is handled by \f[CR]SIG_IGN\f[R]
installed separately, and this handler is not invoked for it.
